<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mubadala Investment Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2002">2002</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mubadala Investment Company is a sovereign wealth fund owned by the government of Abu Dhabi. Founded in 2002 as Mubadala Development Company and merged with the International Petroleum Investment Company in 2017, it invests worldwide in sectors such as energy, technology, aerospace and health care, helping to diversify the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>'s economy away from oil.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The older of Mubadala's two parents, the International Petroleum Investment Company (IPIC), was set up in 1984 to put Abu Dhabi's petroleum wealth to work for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>'s development. Mubadala Development Company followed in 2002 to diversify the economy further. The merger of the two was announced in June 2016, and in 2017 ownership of both passed to a newly created parent, Mubadala Investment Company, a wholly owned investment vehicle of the government of Abu Dhabi, with offices in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. It works through four platforms: direct investments, UAE investments, disruptive investments, and real estate and infrastructure.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its technology holdings grew from the Advanced Technology Investment Company (ATIC) of 2008, which owned the chip foundries GlobalFoundries and Chartered Semiconductor Manufacturing; in 2011 ATIC announced $5.5 billion of investment in chip manufacturing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, and in 2014 it became Mubadala Technology. Mubadala first invested in AMD in 2007 and sold its entire stake in 2019.</p><p>Later purchases included MetrôRio, operator of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> metro, in 2021; a majority stake in Fortress Investment Group, bought from the SoftBank Group in a $3 billion takeover that the Committee on Foreign Investment in the US (Cfius) reviewed over concerns about the UAE's ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and approved in May 2024; and the Canadian asset manager CI Financial Corp., which Mubadala Capital acquired for $8.9 billion in August 2025.</p><p>Some investments drew criticism. Mubadala Capital put €50 million into the Novalpina Capital fund that bought the Israeli security company NSO Group in 2019, at a time when reports said the UAE had used the Pegasus spyware against human rights activists, journalists and Princess Haya. In 2023 the former British minister Gerry Grimstone was criticised for not declaring 13 meetings with Mubadala after becoming an adviser at Investcorp, in which Mubadala had become the biggest external shareholder in 2016.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mubadala Investment Company” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Through the Abu Dhabi Investment Authority, <b>Mubadala</b> and other funds the country became a major investor worldwide.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-arab-emirates">United Arab Emirates · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
